WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.669
A picture four thousand pixels on a side, in one pass,

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:04.821
with no enlarging pass.

00:00:04.821 --> 00:00:07.702
What does the transformer have to do to draw it?

00:00:07.702 --> 00:00:09.718
And can a gaming card keep up?

00:00:12.707 --> 00:00:13.725
Hi, I'm Elowen!

00:00:13.725 --> 00:00:16.779
MiniMax just opened the weights of its image model.

00:00:16.779 --> 00:00:19.833
Let's count what a sixteen megapixel picture costs it.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:21.958
Chapter one.

00:00:21.958 --> 00:00:23.273
From noise to rows.

00:00:27.758 --> 00:00:31.839
The prompt goes through a thirty two billion parameter vision language model,

00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:34.220
and the generator reads its fiftieth layer.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.262
Text rows and noisy image rows are packed into one sequence.

00:00:38.262 --> 00:00:41.554
Fifty transformer blocks attend over all of it at once.

00:00:41.974 --> 00:00:46.699
After six denoising steps, the image decoder turns the latent back into pixels.

00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:49.441
Here is the formula.

00:00:50.317 --> 00:00:53.452
Flow matching draws a straight line from noise to a clean latent.

00:00:53.872 --> 00:00:55.835
At t of one the latent is clean.

00:00:55.835 --> 00:00:57.306
At zero it is pure noise.

00:00:57.726 --> 00:01:01.624
The network learns the velocity along that line, and sampling follows it

00:01:01.624 --> 00:01:04.873
in six distilled steps, each with its own output head.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.160
Start with sixteen megapixels.

00:01:09.323 --> 00:01:12.909
The image encoder shrinks each side sixteen times and keeps twenty

00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:14.213
four channels per spot.

00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:18.542
Every two by two patch becomes one row, so each row covers

00:01:18.542 --> 00:01:20.496
thirty two by thirty two pixels.

00:01:20.496 --> 00:01:23.754
That is sixteen thousand three hundred and eighty four rows.

00:01:24.174 --> 00:01:28.256
Add a five hundred and twelve token prompt, and every block attends

00:01:28.256 --> 00:01:29.957
over almost seventeen thousand rows.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:32.813
Chapter two.

00:01:32.813 --> 00:01:34.721
Why sixteen megapixels is hard.

00:01:39.325 --> 00:01:42.317
Here is a canvas cut into patches, one row each.

00:01:42.317 --> 00:01:45.308
Every row has to compare itself with every other row.

00:01:45.728 --> 00:01:48.975
Double the side, and there are four times the rows.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:50.923
The comparison square grows sixteen times.

00:01:51.343 --> 00:01:54.541
At four thousand pixels a side, attention has grown from a sliver

00:01:54.541 --> 00:01:57.206
of each step to well over a third of it.

00:01:58.925 --> 00:02:02.903
Counted from the config, one step at that size is about a

00:02:02.903 --> 00:02:04.893
thousand and sixty one trillion operations.

00:02:04.893 --> 00:02:06.550
Six steps make the picture.

00:02:08.217 --> 00:02:10.544
And the thirty three billion parameters?

00:02:10.544 --> 00:02:14.424
Thirty nine percent only compute per-step modulation from the timestep.

00:02:14.424 --> 00:02:17.915
With six fixed steps, they collapse into small tables.

00:02:19.883 --> 00:02:20.742
Chapter three.

00:02:20.742 --> 00:02:22.031
References are rows.

00:02:25.592 --> 00:02:27.622
Each reference image goes in twice.

00:02:27.622 --> 00:02:32.020
Once through the vision encoder, as about a thousand tokens beside the prompt.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.897
And once through the image encoder, as latent rows held almost

00:02:35.897 --> 00:02:37.154
clean for every step.

00:02:37.574 --> 00:02:39.141
The target sits after them.

00:02:39.141 --> 00:02:42.902
To the transformer, an edit with three references looks like a short

00:02:42.902 --> 00:02:45.096
video whose last frame is being drawn.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:51.547
Here MiniMax gives it ten references, an empty room and nine catalogue shots.

00:02:51.967 --> 00:02:54.548
One edit puts every piece in the room.

00:02:54.548 --> 00:02:59.064
Up to sixteen references are allowed, and each one adds rows and quadratic cost.

00:03:00.592 --> 00:03:01.342
Chapter four.

00:03:01.342 --> 00:03:02.843
The gaming card question.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:11.121
Stream the twelve gigabyte checkpoint over the card's link every step, and

00:03:11.121 --> 00:03:13.281
the copy takes half a second.

00:03:13.281 --> 00:03:15.442
The arithmetic takes about forty five.

00:03:15.442 --> 00:03:16.882
Offloading hides behind compute.

00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:21.994
On paper it fits, at three to four and a half minutes an image.

00:03:21.994 --> 00:03:23.832
Nobody has timed it on that card.

00:03:23.832 --> 00:03:25.145
And the release has gaps.

00:03:25.565 --> 00:03:28.804
There is no benchmark behind strongest instruction following.

00:03:28.804 --> 00:03:30.424
Only a curated gallery.

00:03:30.844 --> 00:03:36.014
The licence excludes the United States, the European Union, the United Kingdom and Korea.

00:03:36.434 --> 00:03:40.320
And its commercial gate fell from twenty million dollars to one million,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.263
with any hosted service needing permission.

00:03:43.975 --> 00:03:48.010
At sixteen megapixels every block has thousands of rows to chew, so

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:49.355
the weight copies hide.

00:03:49.355 --> 00:03:51.373
The card's arithmetic sets the time.

00:03:53.317 --> 00:03:54.111
To recap.

00:03:54.111 --> 00:03:56.890
Sixteen megapixels is about sixteen thousand rows.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:58.875
References are just more rows.

00:03:58.875 --> 00:04:02.448
And on a gaming card, expect minutes, not seconds.

00:04:02.868 --> 00:04:05.056
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:05.056 --> 00:04:05.681
I'm Elowen.

00:04:05.681 --> 00:04:05.994
Bye!
